explanation:
```sql
CREATE TABLE sessions(id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, team TEXT, done INTEGER, minutes INTEGER);
INSERT INTO sessions VALUES (1,'fern',1,10),(2,'fern',0,20),(3,'moss',0,7);
SELECT team, COUNT(*) AS all_rows, COUNT(*) FILTER (WHERE done=1) AS done_rows, SUM(minutes) FILTER (WHERE done=1) AS done_minutes
FROM sessions
GROUP BY team
ORDER BY team;
```
COUNT(*) counts input rows, so the filtered count for moss is 0. SUM(minutes) adds non-NULL minute values; with no matching inputs it returns NULL, not 0. Fern has 2 total rows but only 1 completed row and 10 completed minutes. FILTER is a clause, not a function call.

```sql
CREATE TABLE sessions(id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, team TEXT, done INTEGER, minutes INTEGER);
INSERT INTO sessions VALUES (1,'fern',1,10),(2,'fern',0,20),(3,'moss',1,7);
SELECT team, COUNT(*) AS all_rows, COUNT(*) FILTER (WHERE done=1) AS done_rows, SUM(minutes) FILTER (WHERE done=1) AS done_minutes
FROM sessions
GROUP BY team
ORDER BY team;
```
Moss now has (all_rows,done_rows,done_minutes)=(1,1,7). Fern is unchanged. Optional practice: set that completed row’s minutes to NULL. Its row still counts, but its value contributes nothing to SUM.

```sql
CREATE TABLE plans(id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, title TEXT);
INSERT INTO plans VALUES (1,'Map'),(2,'Sketch');
BEGIN;
ALTER TABLE plans ADD COLUMN status TEXT NOT NULL DEFAULT 'draft';
ALTER TABLE plans RENAME COLUMN title TO label;
COMMIT;
SELECT id,label,status
FROM plans
ORDER BY id;
```
The result is (1,'Map','draft'),(2,'Sketch','draft'). Existing rows read the new default. NOT NULL rejects missing status values, and DEFAULT supplies a value when it is omitted. SQLite 3.49.1 ADD COLUMN has restrictions: on this populated table, adding a NOT NULL column without a non-NULL default fails. Newer ALTER COLUMN features are not taught here.

```sql
CREATE TABLE plans(id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, title TEXT);
INSERT INTO plans VALUES (1,'Map'),(2,'Sketch');
BEGIN;
ALTER TABLE plans ADD COLUMN status TEXT NOT NULL DEFAULT 'draft';
ALTER TABLE plans RENAME COLUMN title TO label;
ROLLBACK;
SELECT id,title
FROM plans
ORDER BY id;
```
ROLLBACK restores the original title column and removes status. The final query returns (1,Map),(2,Sketch). A failed statement is not an automatic substitute for rolling back a whole multi-statement migration. The supplemental failed-add fixture checks its diagnostic and preserved schema; the learner’s SELECT checks do not grade intentional errors.

